# CAPF and Security Forces of India: Who Guards What

> Every SSC GD, Delhi Police and CGL paper carries a few questions on the
> forces that guard India's borders, cities and airports. They look alike
> (all wear olive or khaki), but each has a different parent ministry,
> raising year, motto and mission. This lesson sorts them into clean
> categories so you can answer in seconds.

## 1. The Six Central Armed Police Forces at a Glance

The Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA) controls the main Central Armed Police Forces (CAPFs). Each is headed by a **Director General (DG)**; the DG is normally an IPS officer, except in the Assam Rifles.

| Force | Raised | Motto | Core role |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| BSF (Border Security Force) | 1 Dec 1965 | Jeevan Paryant Kartavya (Duty unto Death) | Indo-Pakistan and Indo-Bangladesh borders |
| CRPF (Central Reserve Police Force) | 27 Jul 1939 (renamed 28 Dec 1949) | Seva aur Nishtha (Service and Loyalty) | Internal security, anti-Naxal, riot control |
| CISF (Central Industrial Security Force) | 10 Mar 1969 | Sanrakshan evam Suraksha (Protection and Security) | Airports, Delhi Metro, atomic and space sites, Parliament complex |
| ITBP (Indo-Tibetan Border Police) | 24 Oct 1962 | Shaurya, Dridhata, Karma Nishtha (Valour, Determination, Devotion to Duty) | Indo-China border, high altitude |
| SSB (Sashastra Seema Bal) | 15 Mar 1963 (as Special Service Bureau); moved to MHA and renamed 2001 | Service, Security and Brotherhood | Indo-Nepal and Indo-Bhutan borders |
| Assam Rifles | 1835 (as Cachar Levy) | Sentinels of the North East; Friends of the Hill People | Indo-Myanmar border, counter-insurgency in the North East |

[definition]
A **Central Armed Police Force (CAPF)** is a force of the Union government, under the MHA, that is armed, trained and organised to support the police and the border guarding of the country. The six forces above are the usual list for exams; the MHA also groups the **NSG** with them.
[/definition]

## 2. Force-by-Force Notes That Exams Pick

**BSF.** Raised after the 1965 Indo-Pakistan War, so the year 1965 is the anchor. It maintains its own air wing and a water wing, which sets it apart from most other CAPFs. Guards roughly 3,300 km of the Pakistan frontier and about 4,100 km of the Bangladesh frontier.

**CRPF.** The oldest of the regular CAPFs, born as the **Crown Representative's Police** at Neemuch in 1939 and given its present name by the CRPF Act of 1949. Its special units: the **Rapid Action Force (RAF, 1992)**, the **CoBRA** jungle-warfare commandos (raised 2008-09) and women's battalions. **Valour Day is 9 April**, remembering the Sardar Post fight in the Rann of Kutch in 1965.

**CISF.** Created in 1969 under the CISF Act of 1968 to protect public-sector industry. Parliament amended the law in 1983 to make it an armed force of the Union. It runs a fire-service wing and guards airports, seaports, the Delhi Metro, nuclear and space installations and the Taj Mahal.

**ITBP.** Raised right after the 1962 war with China. Its personnel are nicknamed **Himveers** and man posts at around 3,488 km of the India-China border, many above 14,000 feet. Governed by the ITBP Force Act of 1992.

**SSB.** Began as the Special Service Bureau in 1963, designed to train border villagers for resistance if China advanced. After the Kargil review it moved to the MHA in 2001 and became the Sashastra Seema Bal. Guards about 1,751 km (Nepal) and 699 km (Bhutan).

**Assam Rifles.** The **oldest paramilitary force** of India (1835). It is called the "Sentinels of the North East". It is the unusual one: the MHA administers it, but the **Indian Army holds operational control**, and its DG is a **Lieutenant General** from the Army. Border: Indo-Myanmar (about 1,643 km).

### Memory trick for borders

| Border | Force |
| --- | --- |
| Pakistan, Bangladesh | BSF |
| China | ITBP |
| Nepal, Bhutan | SSB |
| Myanmar | Assam Rifles |
| Coast | Indian Coast Guard (MoD) and Navy |

## 3. Special and Counter-Terror Forces

| Body | Raised / Act | Ministry | Motto or note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| NSG (National Security Guard) | 16 Oct 1984; NSG Act 1986 | MHA | Sarvatra Sarvottam Suraksha (Best Security Everywhere); "Black Cats" |
| NDRF (National Disaster Response Force) | 19 Jan 2006; Disaster Management Act 2005 | MHA | Aapda Seva Sadaiv Sarvatra |
| SPG (Special Protection Group) | 1985; SPG Act 1988 | Cabinet Secretariat | Shauryam, Samarpanam, Surakshanam; protects the Prime Minister |
| RPF (Railway Protection Force) | RPF Act 1957 (origins 1872) | Ministry of Railways | Yasho Labhasva (Attain honour) |

The **NSG** was formed after the Operation Blue Star period. Its famous operations include **Black Thunder** (Golden Temple, 1988) and **Black Tornado** (Mumbai attacks, 2008). Its main hub is at **Manesar**. The **SPG** is a close-protection unit and is **not** under the MHA, which is a favourite trap. The **NDRF** is the only dedicated disaster-response force of its kind; some BSF, CRPF, CISF and ITBP battalions are linked to it.

## 4. Investigation and Intelligence Agencies

| Agency | Year | Parent | Head's title |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| IB (Intelligence Bureau) | 1887 (Central Special Branch) | MHA | Director, IB |
| RAW (Research and Analysis Wing) | 1968 | Cabinet Secretariat | Secretary (Research) |
| CBI (Central Bureau of Investigation) | 1963 (roots in SPE of 1941) | Ministry of Personnel (DoPT) | Director |
| NIA (National Investigation Agency) | Act 2008; began working 2009 | MHA | Director General |

Points to hold on to:

- **IB** is internal intelligence and counter-intelligence; **RAW** is external intelligence. R.N. Kao was RAW's first chief.
- **CBI** derives its powers from the **Delhi Special Police Establishment Act, 1946**. Its Director is chosen by a committee of the **Prime Minister, the Leader of the Opposition and the Chief Justice of India (or a Supreme Court judge nominated by him)**. Motto: "Industry, Impartiality, Integrity".
- **NIA** was set up after the **26/11 Mumbai attacks** to probe terror and related offences; the 2019 amendment widened its jurisdiction to cases such as human trafficking and cyber-terrorism, including offences abroad against Indian interests.

## 5. Coast Guard and the Armed Forces Link

The **Indian Coast Guard** works under the **Ministry of Defence**, not the MHA. It started as an interim force in February 1977 and was formally established under the **Coast Guard Act, 1978** (18 August 1978). Its motto is **Vayam Rakshamah** ("We Protect"), its head is a **Director General** and it is headquartered in New Delhi. **Coast Guard Day is 1 February.**

[example]
A question reads: "Which force is under the Ministry of Defence?" The options are BSF, CISF, Indian Coast Guard, ITBP. The first, second and fourth are MHA forces; only the Coast Guard reports to the MoD. If the question asked instead for "operational control by the Indian Army", the answer would flip to the Assam Rifles.
[/example]

## 6. Commonly Confused Pairs

- **CRPF vs CISF:** CRPF = internal law and order and anti-insurgency (1939); CISF = industrial and infrastructure security (1969).
- **SSB vs ITBP:** SSB covers Nepal and Bhutan; ITBP covers China.
- **IB vs RAW:** IB is inward-facing under the MHA; RAW is outward-facing under the Cabinet Secretariat.
- **CBI vs NIA:** CBI investigates corruption and major crime and is under DoPT; NIA investigates terror and is under the MHA.
- **RPF vs GRP:** the RPF belongs to the Railways; the Government Railway Police belongs to the state governments.

[summary]
Six CAPFs sit under the MHA: BSF (1965, Duty unto Death), CRPF (1939/1949, Service and Loyalty), CISF (1969, Protection and Security), ITBP (1962, Valour-Determination-Devotion to Duty), SSB (1963/2001, Service-Security-Brotherhood) and Assam Rifles (1835, army-led operationally). NSG (1984) is the counter-terror commando force; the SPG (1985/1988) protects the PM and sits under the Cabinet Secretariat; the NDRF (2006) handles disasters; the RPF is a Railways force. IB (1887) and NIA (2008 Act) are with the MHA, RAW (1968) with the Cabinet Secretariat, CBI (1963) with DoPT, and the Coast Guard (1978) with the MoD.
[/summary]
